 "prompt_sha256": "275c9be19279b929f495bf64d66752d53c3d9d0df4d1971f974b8e9825f41fb3",
 "answer": "To start the Ollama container with access to all NVIDIA GPUs, you need to configure Docker to use the NVIDIA driver and then start the container with the appropriate options. Here's how you can do it:\n\n1. **Configure Docker to Use the NVIDIA Driver:**\n\n   First, ensure that Docker is configured to use the NVIDIA driver by running the following commands:\n\n   ```shell\n   sudo nvidia-ctk runtime configure --runtime=docker\n   sudo systemctl restart docker\n   ```\n\n   This step is necessary to allow Docker containers to access the NVIDIA GPUs. These instructions are found in the document: \"Configure Docker to use Nvidia driver\" [ollama/docs/docker.md (lines 45-61)].\n\n2. **Start the Ollama Container:**\n\n   After configuring Docker, you can start the Ollama container with access to all available NVIDIA GPUs using the following command:\n\n   ```shell\n   docker run -d --gpus=all -v ollama:/root/.ollama -p 11434:11434 --name ollama ollama/ollama\n   ```\n\n   This command starts the container in detached mode (`-d`), assigns all available GPUs (`--gpus=all`), mounts a volume for persistent data (`-v ollama:/root/.ollama`), maps the port 11434 on the host to port 11434 in the container (`-p 11434:11434`), and names the container `ollama`. These details are specified in the document: \"Start the container\" [ollama/docs/docker.md (lines 45-61)].\n\nBy following these steps, you should be able to start the Ollama container with access to all NVIDIA GPUs.",
